{
  "order": 20,
  "title": "Grandfather Clock",
  "hook": "A falling weight does all the work; the pendulum only lets it out, half a tooth every second.",
  "category": "time",
  "summary": [
    "A longcase clock keeps a household’s time to the second for a week on one winding, with nothing to tend but a key. It runs on gravity: a weight sinking slowly down the case supplies the energy, and a pendulum a metre long decides how fast it may be spent.",
    "The weight hangs on a gut line wound round a barrel. The barrel’s great wheel drives a train of wheels: the centre wheel, which carries the minute hand round once an hour, then the third wheel, then the escape wheel, whose 30 teeth go round once a minute. The anchor rocks with the pendulum and lets the escape wheel on half a tooth at each swing, and the motion work behind the dial turns the hour hand 12 times slower than the minute hand.",
    "A pendulum 994 mm long swings once a second whatever its bob weighs, because its period depends only on its length. The weight sinks about 6.7 mm an hour; at each tick a tooth gives the pendulum a small push to make up what the air took. On this model the pendulum swings, the train ticks and the hands keep your time. The pocket watch does the same job with a coiled spring and a balance wheel beating six times a second."
  ],
